WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.960
There are lots of good reasons
to try and follow a healthier diet—

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.040
you lose weight,
you feel good,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.840
but the main reason?

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.360
To live a longer, happier,
more productive life.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.400
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.600
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.040
Today we look at how the brain’s
detox mechanism works—

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.720
the glymphatic system—
and more importantly,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.920
what we can do to make
it work even better.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.720
The glymphatic system is a newly
discovered drainage system

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.320
in the brain that clears the brain
of toxic waste byproducts.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.200
Sleep is a great mystery.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.080
A trait shared
across animal species,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.160
sleep must be
of vital importance

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.120
to survive
natural selection pressures

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.240
to eliminate
such a vulnerable state.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.760
Indeed, cringeworthy experiments
have shown

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.600
that keeping animals awake
long enough

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.560
can be fatal within
eleven to thirty-two days.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.840
It turns out
sleep is of-the-brain,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.760
by the brain,
and for the brain.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.040
One function of sleep that has
been elucidated in recent years

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.320
is the clearance
of toxic waste byproducts

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.520
through a newly discovered
drainage system in the brain.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.120
With the invention
of the encephalogram, EEG,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.040
to measure brain wave activity,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.320
the scientific world
was quickly disabused

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.560
of the notion that sleep was
a time of rest for the brain.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.200
During certain stages of sleep

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.120
there is brain-wide activity
going on,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.360
but what was
the brain actively doing?

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.440
More than 2,000 years ago,

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.600
Aristotle proposed that sleep
helps the body clean the blood.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.640
Today, we know sleep may help
the body clean the brain.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.680
Until 2012, we thought
that the brain was singular

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.440
among organs for recycling
nearly all of its own waste.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.160
It had to,
since it was separated

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.840
from the rest of the body
by the blood-brain barrier.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.800
But the barrier that keeps
toxins out of the brain

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.200
presumably keeps toxins in.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.720
Then, in 2012,

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.840
a brain-wide
fluid transport network

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.280
was discovered,
termed the glymphatic system.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.680
By microscopically tracking dye

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.200
injected into the brains
of mice,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.920
scientists discovered
fluid-filled tunnels

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.240
surrounding blood vessels
in the brain.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.480
The pressure wave
of arterial pulses

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.240
with every heartbeat
milks the fluid along

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.000
before eventually draining
into the cerebrospinal fluid

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.320
surrounding the brain.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.680
What does this
have to do with sleep?

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.600
The whole system is only
really active when sleeping.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.440
During wakefulness,
these tunnels are clamped down,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.840
reducing glymphatic flow
by 90 percent.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.360
The thought is the fluid shifts
might interfere

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.320
with targeted neurotransmitter
chemical communication

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.880
in the awake state.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.840
So the biological need for sleep
may reflect the need

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.440
for the brain to enter
into a state to filter out

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.680
potentially neurotoxic waste
products like beta amyloid,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.920
which is implicated
in Alzheimer's disease.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.680
Perhaps this could help explain

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.480
why those who routinely get
fewer than seven hours of sleep

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.880
a night are at increased risk

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.600
of developing cognitive
disorders such as dementia.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.800
Randomizing individuals
to have their sleep disrupted

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.160
by a series of beeps

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.880
administered through headphones
in a sleep lab

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.760
increases amyloid levels,
whereas improving sleep,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.760
by treating sleep apnea patients
with CPAP, for example,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.400
improves slow wave activity,

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.040
deep sleep, and appears
to lower amyloid levels.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.880
PET scans show
even a single all-nighter

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.320
can cause
a significant increase

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.160
in accumulation of beta amyloid
in critical brain areas.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.960
The problem is that
glymphatic brain filtration

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.640
appears to decline with aging.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.560
Old mice only have
10 to 20 percent

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.240
the glymphatic function
of young mice.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.080
This could be due
to a number of factors.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.000
As we age,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.200
we experience less
of the deep, slow wave sleep,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.560
the type of sleep during
which brain waste clearance

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.000
appears to be the most active.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.640
Further contributing
to the stagnancy,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.400
our arteries tend
to stiffen as we age,

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.640
reducing the pulsations
that drive the glymphatic pump.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.880
That also offers
one potential explanation

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.640
as to why hypertension
is tied to dementia.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.240
The thickening of artery walls
with high blood pressure

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.400
also has a stiffening effect.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.320
How can we counter this
age-related glymphatic decline

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.600
and keep our brains cleaner?

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.017
We'll explore
just that question, next.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.702
What can we do to prevent the decline
in glymphatic brain filtration as we age?

00:04:41.702 --> 00:04:44.036
Let’s find out. 

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.720
Other than getting enough sleep,

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.080
what can we do to improve
the glymphatic clearance

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.480
of waste from our brains?

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.400
The provision
of a running wheel,

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.800
so mice could
voluntarily exercise,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.040
has been shown to improve
glymphatic clearance

00:04:58.040 --> 00:04:59.160
in aging mice,

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.280
which was accompanied
by a reduced buildup

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:04.960
of amyloid deposits
and improved cognition.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.680
Sleeping position
may also make a difference.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.920
Studies on rats show that
their natural sleeping position,

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.600
curled up on their sides,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.680
allows for better
glymphatic transport

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.240
than sleeping on their backs
or stomachs.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.720
People also tend
to spend most of their time

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.280
sleeping on their side,

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.800
particularly their right side
versus left,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.440
compared to their backs
or stomachs.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.080
This may maximize blood outflow
from the brain.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.760
When we sleep
on our right side,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.840
our right internal jugular vein,

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.680
the main blood vessel
in our neck

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.560
draining blood from the head,
is wide open,

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:40.840
and our left jugular

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.200
is partially collapsed
and vice versa.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.480
Since most people have
a dominant right jugular vein,

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.680
sleeping on our right side
might maximize brain drainage.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.920
Does it matter?

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.760
Well, people with
neurodegenerative disease,

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.800
mostly mild cognitive impairment
and Alzheimer's disease,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.040
tend to sleep more
on their backs

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.280
than those
with normal cognition.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.760
About seventy-two percent
spent at least two hours a night

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.760
on their backs compared
to 37 percent of those

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.280
with healthier brains,

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.240
raising
"the intriguing possibility

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.600
that head position
during sleep

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.800
could influence the clearing
of neurotoxic proteins

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.080
from the brain."

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.800
In crib death,
sudden infant death syndrome,

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.080
sleeping position
can be a life-saver,

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.800
leading to slogans like
"back to sleep,"

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.000
or more morbidly,
"face up to wake up."

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.440
It's premature
for an adult jingle,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.200
maybe, on your flank
to not draw a blank?

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.080
The characteristic position
of poor sleepers

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.200
is on their back, though,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.800
so maybe it's the poor sleeping

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.760
rather than the position,
per se,

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:50.680
that leads to cognitive decline,

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.800
or the causality
could be reversed,

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.320
with dementia deteriorating
good sleep habits.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.840
Even if sleeping position
did matter,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.120
it may take a night
in a sleep lab

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.400
to track your movements.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.600
It turns out self-reported
sleep positions are often false.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.680
Should brain benefits to side
sleeping ever be established,

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.120
you can train yourself with
so-called "positional therapies"

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.640
such as
the "tennis ball technique,"

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.760
which involves wearing a shirt
to bed backwards,

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.320
with a ball stuffed
in the chest pocket.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:27.520
The uncertainties don't end
with sleeping position.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.520
The glymphatic mechanism itself

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.760
was rapidly embraced
in scientific circles

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:33.960
and the popular press,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.360
however,
it's been controversial.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.680
It wasn't until 2019

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.200
that the first evidence
was published

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.040
that the glymphatic system
discovered in rodents

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.040
even existed in human brains.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.600
Even the relationship between
sleep and Alzheimer's disease

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.400
is perhaps best summed up
in a recent neurology review

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.440
entitled "It's complicated."

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.800
Yes, those getting
less than 7 hours of sleep

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.520
may have higher rates
of Alzheimer's,

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.320
but those getting more than 8
are at higher risk too.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:05.280
If anything,

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.120
population studies show
that longer sleep durations,

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.640
more than 8 or 9 hours,

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.200
are more strongly linked
to Alzheimer's disease

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.280
and dementia in general

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.680
than sleeping less than
5 or 6 hours.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:20.400
The association between dementia
and long sleep duration

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.200
could be reverse causation,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.680
where prodromal changes
in the brain

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.600
years before
Alzheimer's is diagnosed

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.960
causes prolonged sleep.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:32.440
Long sleep duration may also
just be a confounding factor,

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.800
a marker of some
underlying health problem

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.520
that's the real culprit.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.920
For example, oversleeping
may be a sign of depression,

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:43.720
which itself is an established
risk factor for dementia.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.320
But there is a plausible
biological mechanism

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.360
for how extended sleep duration

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.400
could increase
dementia risk directly.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:53.800
Longer sleep duration,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.120
typically defined as sleeping
more than 8 hours a night,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.960
is associated with signs
of systemic inflammation,

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:04.880
elevated levels of C-reactive
protein and Interleukin 6,

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:06.960
and both of those
inflammatory markers

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.880
in turn are associated with
an increase in dementia risk.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.840
So much more
needs to be teased out

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.960
about the role
of the glymphatic system

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:18.537
before we make conscious efforts
to tweak it.

